.TH LINUXCTL 1 "September 2026" "linux-mcp-daemon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linuxctl \- Command line interface for the Linux MCP Daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIresources\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIresource <uri>\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fI<group>/<command>\fR [\fIcommand-options\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linuxctl
is the client utility for interacting with the Linux Model Context Protocol (MCP) Daemon (mcpd). It allows users and AI agents to send commands to the daemon running in the background.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-server \ \fIURL\fR
The URL of the mcpd server to connect to. Defaults to http://localhost:9091.
.TP
.BR \-token \ \fITOKEN\fR
Bearer token for authentication. If not provided via this flag, the client will look for the \fBMCP_TOKEN\fR environment variable.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ping
Pings the mcpd daemon to verify connectivity and authentication. Returns a success message if the daemon is reachable and the token is valid.
.TP
.B resources
Fetches and lists all statically available resources and resource templates from the daemon.
.TP
.B resource <uri>
Reads the exact contents of an MCP resource. Use \fB--output\fR to optionally format JSON output as \fBtable\fR or \fBjson\fR.
.TP
.B <group>/<command>
Dynamically executes an MCP tool exposed by the daemon. For example, \fBfiles/list\fR or \fBdisks/free\fR. Run \fBlinuxctl\fR without arguments to see available groups, or \fBlinuxctl <group>\fR to see available commands within a group.
.TP
.B [command-options]
Arguments specific to the command being executed. You can provide these either positionally (e.g., \fBlinuxctl files/list /var/log\fR) or as explicit flags (e.g., \fB--path /var/log --privileged true\fR). You can also use \fB--output table\fR or \fB--output json\fR to format structural data.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MCP_TOKEN
The bearer token used for authenticating with the daemon. Can be used instead of the \fB\-token\fR flag.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ping the daemon using the default server and a specific token:
.IP
.B linuxctl \-token "my-test-token-123" ping
.PP
Ping the daemon using an environment variable:
.IP
.B export MCP_TOKEN="my-test-token-123"
.br
.B linuxctl ping
.PP
List all available resources:
.IP
.B linuxctl resources
.PP
Read the system uname as a resource:
.IP
.B linuxctl resource os://uname
.PP
Read connected USB devices as a formatted table:
.IP
.B linuxctl resource devices://usb --output table
.PP
Execute a dynamic command with positional arguments:
.IP
.B linuxctl files/list /var/log
.PP
Execute a dynamic command with explicit flags:
.IP
.B linuxctl disks/free --path / --privileged true
.SH AUTHOR
Antigravity
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mcpd (8)
